      </div>

        <div id="curve-and-resistance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>A Steep Curve and a Magnetic Brake: What They Mean</h2>
<p>
            Curved trainers differ mainly in how deep the curve is. A shallow curve, such as TrueForm's,
            gives little help and rewards a disciplined stride; a deep one makes the belt eager to run
            away when you lean forward. Bells of Steel describes the Blitz's 17-degree curve as steep, and
            Treadmill Review Guru calls it "a high curve" that you feel almost immediately. That suits the
            Blitz's purpose: hard accelerations and short sprints, where a willing belt helps you reach top
            speed. It is less suited to long steady runs; one review notes that distance runners may not
            find it suitable for steady-state work, and the same is true of most deep-curved decks. Our <a href="/curved-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill guide</a> explains how curve depth changes the feel.
          </p>
          <p>
            The magnetic brake is what separates the Blitz from the AssaultRunner and the TrueForm
            machines. A lever on the right of the frame moves magnets closer to a flywheel, which makes
            the belt harder to turn without any friction pad to wear out. At low settings that steadies
            the belt for walking and jogging; at high settings it turns the treadmill into a resisted
            trainer. Bells of Steel pitches the top levels for sled pushes, bracing against the padded
            front handles and driving the belt with short, powerful steps, and reviewers describe the
            heavier settings as feeling more like a strength session than a run.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical result is range. On one machine you can sprint at level one, do resisted
            accelerations at the middle levels and grind out sled-style pushes at the top, which a
            no-resistance curved trainer cannot do without a separate tether or sled. If those sessions
            are the reason you want a curved treadmill, the Blitz makes sense. If you mostly want to run,
            resistance is a feature you will rarely use. For sessions that fit either kind of deck, see our <a href="/curved-treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill workouts</a> and the <a href="/treadmill-sprint-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill sprint workout</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Console and Tracking</h2>
<p>
            The Blitz's console runs on batteries, so the machine needs no outlet. Treadmill Review Guru
            lists time, pace, distance, speed, calories, watts, resistance level and heart rate, with heart
            rate needing a compatible ANT+ chest strap; Bells of Steel's table lists "5kHz" connectivity,
            the older wireless standard used by some gym chest straps. Neither source describes Bluetooth
            FTMS, so do not expect the Blitz to pair with <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Zwift</a> or a treadmill app the way some newer
            curved trainers do. If app tracking matters, a <a href="/best-foot-pods-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running foot pod</a> will give your watch or app pace and distance on any treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            The one quirk the review flags is that you have to press start before the console tracks time
            and distance; step on and run without pressing it and the session is not recorded. Calorie and
            watt figures on any manual treadmill are estimates, and our piece on <a href="/are-treadmill-calories-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calorie accuracy</a> explains why a heart-rate strap gives a better guide to effort.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivery-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Delivery, Space and Floors</h2>
<p>
            Bells of Steel ships the Blitz by freight only, free to the contiguous US, and offers pickup
            from its Indianapolis location. The box is 73 by 33 by 23 inches and 355 lb. Bells of Steel's
            listing says plainly that the unit is large and heavy and recommends a ground-floor install
            rather than a basement or an upper floor, and suggests bringing a friend if you collect it.
            Take that seriously: a 325 lb machine on a staircase is a two- or three-person job with a
            proper dolly. Our <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to moving a treadmill</a> covers the method.
          </p>
          <p>
            Set up, the Blitz is 70 inches long, 30 wide and 62 high, a little longer than the TrueForm
            machines and about the same as the AssaultRunner Pro. It does not fold. Leave clearance behind
            it for safety, put it on a level, solid floor with a heavy rubber mat, and remember the 13-inch
            step-up the review reports, which is higher than most motorized treadmills. A garage is an
            ideal home, since there is no motor or electronics to worry about in the cold; see <a href="/best-treadmills-for-garage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our garage treadmill picks</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Warranty, Returns and Recalls</h2>
<p>
            Bells of Steel's specification table and the independent review agree on five years on the
            frame and one year on parts. That is the weakest term among the curved trainers we compare
            here: TrueForm gives five years on moving parts, and Assault three on the AssaultRunner Pro's.
            The one-year parts term matters more than the frame term on a curved trainer, because the
            parts that wear are the bearings and the slats. Ask Bells of Steel what "parts" includes, and
            whether labour is covered, before you buy; our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> lists the questions.
          </p>
          <p>
            The US store offers 30-day returns. On a freight item, find out who pays return freight before
            you order, because on a 355 lb crate that can be a large sum. We searched the US Consumer
            Product Safety Commission's recall database for Bells of Steel on 5 November 2026 and found no
            recalls.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bells-of-steel-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Other Bells of Steel Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            The Blitz is the most expensive of three treadmills on Bells of Steel's US store. On
            5 November 2026 it also listed the Dreadmill at $1,849.99 (stated compare-at $2,349.99), a
            flat, magnetic-resistance machine that Bells of Steel sells as a farmer's-walk, sled-push and
            manual-incline trainer in one, and the Wooden Residential Manual Treadmill at $1,799.99
            (compare-at $1,999.99), a curved trainer with a gentler 8.4-degree curve and a wooden finish.
            Earlier coverage also mentions a Blitz Mountain Climber treadmill, which was not in the store's
            treadmill collection on the day we checked. We have not cross-checked the Dreadmill or Wooden
            model figures, so they are mentioned here for context rather than recommended.
          </p>
          <p>
            Of the three, the Blitz is the one for sprinting and resisted running. The Dreadmill is a
            strength-conditioning tool more than a running machine, and the Wooden model is the home-runner
            option with a calmer curve at well under half the price. For the broader flat and budget end of
            the category, see our <a href="/best-manual-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best manual treadmills</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Blitz vs AssaultRunner Pro, SOLE C80 and TrueForm Trainer</h2>
<p>
            Three curved trainers are the obvious comparisons, and each beats the Blitz on something.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">&nbsp;</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Blitz</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">AssaultRunner Pro</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">SOLE C80</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">TrueForm Trainer</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Price, Nov 2026</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,749.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,499</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,399.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,495–$3,995</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Surface</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">65 x 17 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">62 x 17 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">62 x 19 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">17 in. wide; 54 in. on Rogue's listing</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Resistance</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 magnetic levels</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8 magnetic levels</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Max user</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">350 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">330 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Parts warranty</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 years moving parts</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 years moving parts</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>The SOLE C80</strong> is the closest like-for-like rival: a curved deck with magnetic
            resistance, eight levels rather than six, and a belt two inches wider, for $2,350 less. It gives
            up 70 lb of user capacity and three inches of <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>deck length</a>, and it has the same one-year parts
            term. For most home buyers who want a resisted curved trainer, it is the better buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The AssaultRunner Pro</strong> has no resistance but costs $1,250 less, carries a
            longer moving-parts warranty and has the largest base of independent reviews of any curved
            trainer. If sled pushes are a small part of your plan, it is the safer choice. Our <a href="/assaultrunner-pro-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AssaultRunner Pro review</a> covers it in detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The TrueForm Trainer</strong> matches the Blitz's 400 lb rating and has a much shallower
            curve and a longer warranty, but no resistance. It is the pick for steady runners and form work
            rather than sprinting; see our <a href="/trueform-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">TrueForm buyer's guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy the Blitz</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy the Blitz if</strong> you train for speed and power — field sports, sprinting,
            combat sports or conditioning — and want one machine that sprints, resists and pushes like a
            sled; if you weigh more than the 330 to 350 lb that cheaper curved trainers allow; or if you
            are already buying a Bells of Steel rack and like the idea of one freight delivery and one
            supplier.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere if</strong> you mostly run at a steady pace (a shallower curve or a
            motorized treadmill will be more comfortable), if you mainly walk (see our <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual vs motorized comparison</a>),
            or if price matters, since the SOLE C80 delivers resisted curved running for about half the
            money. And if you live upstairs, Bells of Steel's own advice is to install it on a ground floor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Bells of Steel Blitz</h2>
          <p>The Blitz is a specialised machine with a high price and freight delivery. These are the ways buyers end up with the wrong one.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Paying for resistance you will not use</h3>
              <p>The magnetic brake is the Blitz’s reason to exist. If your plan is steady runs and intervals rather than sled pushes and resisted sprints, a no-resistance curved trainer such as the AssaultRunner Pro does the same running job for about $1,250 less.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Planning delivery around the machine weight, not the box</h3>
              <p>The Blitz is 325 lb, but it arrives as a 355 lb crate measuring 73 by 33 by 23 inches. Measure doorways and stairs against the crate, check whether the carrier brings it inside, and follow Bells of Steel’s advice to install it on a ground floor.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Expecting a shallow curve for long runs</h3>
              <p>A 17-degree curve is built to accelerate. Runners who want long, steady sessions often find a steep curve busy, and reviewers say the Blitz suits sprinting better than distance. If distance running is the goal, a shallower curve such as TrueForm’s or a motorized treadmill fits better.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not reading the one-year parts warranty</h3>
              <p>Five years on the frame sounds reassuring, but the parts that wear on a curved trainer are bearings and slats, covered for one year. Ask what parts and labour cover includes, and who pays return freight within the 30-day window, before you order.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">The Bells of Steel Blitz is a capable curved trainer for speed and
          power work: a 65-inch, 400 lb-rated deck with a steep 17-degree curve and six magnetic
          resistance levels, at $4,749.99 on the day we checked.</strong> Its weaknesses are the price
          and a one-year parts warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            For most buyers who want resistance on a curved deck, the SOLE C80 does the job for about half
          the money; for pure sprinting, the AssaultRunner Pro costs $1,250 less. Compare them all in our <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best curved treadmills</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

