        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How much is the Bells of Steel Blitz treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On 5 November 2026 Bells of Steel’s US store listed the Blitz Magnetic Resistance Manual Treadmill at $4,749.99, against a stated price of $4,999.99, with free freight to the contiguous US. Earlier in 2026 it was listed at $4,999.99. Prices change, so check before ordering.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the weight limit on the Bells of Steel Blitz?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Bells of Steel and an independent review both give a 400 lb maximum user weight. The machine itself weighs 325 lb; the 355 lb figure sometimes quoted is the boxed shipping weight.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Blitz a curved treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. It is a self-powered curved treadmill with a 17-degree curve, which Bells of Steel describes as steep. Lean forward and the belt speeds up; drift back and it slows. Its six-level magnetic brake adds resistance on top.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does the Bells of Steel Blitz need electricity?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. The belt is driven by you and the console runs on batteries, so it needs no outlet. That makes it a good fit for a garage, but Bells of Steel recommends a ground-floor install because of its weight.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What warranty comes with the Blitz?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Bells of Steel’s specification table gives five years on the frame and one year on parts, and an independent review gives the same terms. Ask whether labour is included before you buy.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Blitz better than the AssaultRunner Pro?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It does more: six resistance levels, a 400 lb rating and a longer deck. The AssaultRunner Pro costs about $1,250 less and has a longer parts warranty. If you want resisted sprints and sled pushes, the Blitz or the cheaper SOLE C80 fits; if you mainly sprint, the AssaultRunner is the safer buy. We have not tested either.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
